SUMMARY
Stiff- Person syndrome (SPS) is a rare autoimmune 
neurological disorder characterised by episodic painful 
muscle rigidity and violent spasms. A significant trigger 
for the painful spasms experienced by patients is pain 
itself, making optimal pain management and avoidance 
a necessity. While first- line and second- line therapies 
for spasm prevention and termination are known, there 
is a paucity of evidence to guide pain management. 
We report the case of a 26- year- old woman with SPS 
referred for excruciating muscle cramping and rigidity 
with pain lasting beyond the episodes themselves. 
We report the novel use of ketamine and intravenous 
magnesium sulfate which may provide analgesia, spasm 
avoidance and early termination of exacerbations in SPS.
BACKGROUND
Stiff- Person syndrome (SPS) is a rare acquired auto-
immune neurological disorder affecting an esti-
mated 1 per 1 million individuals globally and is 
characterised by episodic painful muscle rigidity 
and violent spasms. 1 While the exact pathophys -
iology of SPS remains unclear, the most common 
association is with anti- glutamic acid immunoglob-
ulins (anti- GAD65), which are produced against 
cytoplasmic enzymes involved in gamma aminobu-
tyric acid (GABA) synthesis in the central nervous 
system (CNS).2 In the presence of these antibodies, 
patients experience rigidity and tetanus- like painful 
spasms triggered by pain, tactile and emotional 
stimuli.3
The aims of SPS management are symptom 
relief and improvement in quality of life. As such, 
avoiding spasms and rigidity as well as pain manage-
ment are the prime focus of therapy. 1 It has been 
recognised that a significant trigger for the painful 
spasms experienced by patients with SPS is pain 
itself, crystallising the importance of optimal pain 
management and prevention.4 T raditional pharma-
cotherapeutic agents for SPS include the GABA- A 
agonist, benzodiazepines, and the GABA- B agonist, 
baclofen, as the first- line agents to reduce muscle 
spasms and rigidity. For medium- term disease modi-
fication, there is evidence supporting the use of 
intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG), plasmapher -
esis, steroids and immunomodulators such as ritux-
imab.5 Due to the rarity of SPS, there is a paucity 
of high- quality evidence to substantiate many of 
the additional treatments that have been proposed 
in cases unresponsive to the therapies aforemen-
tioned. Where benzodiazepines are insufficient to 
alleviate painful spasms at doses not causing signif -
icant sedation, gabapentin and valproate have been 
documented to provide symptomatic relief, likely 
via their actions on the GABAergic pathway. 6 7 In 
cases of severe pain secondary to SPS, opioids and 
clonidine have also been reportedly used.8 9 Despite 
documentation of multiple agents being used in 
SPS, there is insufficient evidence to support their 
routine use, and in the absence of clear guidelines, 
treatments must be tailored to the patient and their 
responsiveness to therapy.
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">CASE PRESENTATION
A 26- year- old woman with SPS was referred to our 
acute pain service for excruciating bilateral hand 
and truncal spasms and severe pain unresponsive 
to conventional first- line and second- line therapies. 
This occurred weeks following a clinical diagnosis 
of SPS, occurring on a background of controlled 
Addison’s disease, systemic lupus erythematosus 
and Hashimoto’s thyroiditis. Anti- GAD65 anti-
bodies were elevated in the serum (38.6 U/mL, refer-
ence range <5 U/mL) but were consistently negative 
in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). Extended panels of 
autoimmune and paraneoplastic antineuronal anti-
bodies, including amphiphysin, glycine receptor, 
dipeptidyl- peptidase- like protein 6 (DPPX) and 
N- methyl- D- aspartate (NMDA) receptor antibodies, 
were also negative in the serum and CSF, although 
at the height of her illness the patient had a CSF 
lymphocytosis which normalised as the patient’s 
symptoms improved. The patient had been diag-
nosed during a recent admission to the neurology 
unit where she was treated with high- dose intrave-
nous steroids, IVIG, plasmapheresis and rituximab. 
She was discharged with 2 mg clonazepam four 
times daily, 50 mg baclofen two times daily, 25 µg 
clonidine three times daily, 400 mg gabapentin four 
times daily, 100 mg immediate release tapentadol 
three times daily and 1 g paracetamol three times 
daily for symptomatic treatment (figure 1). Four 
days after discharge, the patient re- presented after 
five episodes of debilitating truncal spasms, rigidity 
and a baseline pain score of 8 out of 10. T riggers 
for her spasms included anxiety and anger, as well 
as rapid movements, upper limb muscle tension and 
notably, inadequately controlled pain. As such, the 
patient was referred to the acute pain service in 
order to optimise her pain control with a view to 
avoid spasms.
Early in her second admission, the patient was 
given an additional 50 µg dose of clonidine and 
25 mg baclofen at night for improved nocturnal 
control of symptoms. With pain unchanged on day 
three of admission, as well as three salvos of severe 
spasms, clonazepam dosing was increased from four 
to five times daily in conjunction with 100 mg oral</seg>

